<article class="env-lemma" id="04T5">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04T5">Lemma <span data-tag="04T5">94.16.2</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme contained in $\mathit{Sch}_{fppf}$. Let $\mathcal{X}$ be an algebraic stack over $S$. Let $U$ be an algebraic space over $S$. Let $f : \mathcal{S}_ U \to \mathcal{X}$ be a surjective smooth morphism. Let $(U, R, s, t, c)$ be the groupoid in algebraic spaces and $f_{can} : [U/R] \to \mathcal{X}$ be the result of applying Lemma <a href="/tag/04T4" data-tag="04T4">94.16.1</a> to $U$ and $f$. Then </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
the morphisms $s$, $t$ are smooth, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
the $1$-morphism $f_{can} : [U/R] \to \mathcal{X}$ is an equivalence. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The morphisms $s, t$ are smooth by Lemmas <a href="/tag/0459" data-tag="0459">94.10.2</a> and <a href="/tag/045A" data-tag="045A">94.10.3</a>. As the $1$-morphism $f$ is smooth and surjective it is clear that given any scheme $T$ and any object $a \in \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\mathcal{X}_ T)$ there exists a smooth and surjective morphism $T' \to T$ such that $a|_ T'$ comes from an object of $[U/R]_{T'}$. Since $f_{can} : [U/R] \to \mathcal{X}$ is fully faithful, we deduce that $[U/R] \to \mathcal{X}$ is essentially surjective as descent data on objects are effective on both sides, see Stacks, Lemma <a href="/tag/046N" data-tag="046N">8.4.8</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>